<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristides</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 530–468 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Aristides the Just</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristides, called the Just, was an Athenian statesman and general. He fought at Marathon in 490 BCE, was ostracised in 482 after opposing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>, and was recalled to command at Salamis and Plataea in 480–479. In 478–477 he assessed the contributions of ships or money owed by the members of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a>, and his fairness gave him his lasting nickname.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristides.jpg" alt="Aristides" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Another Believer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boston_(2019)_-_194.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aristides came from a family of moderate fortune, and his father was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>. Early in life he became a follower of the statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a>. He probably first came to notice as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> in command of his own tribe, Antiochis, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon">Battle of Marathon</a> in 490 BCE. Because of the distinction he won there, he is said to have been elected archon eponymos for the next year. He followed a conservative policy meant to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> a land power, and he was one of the chief opponents of the naval policy proposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The conflict between the two leaders ended in the ostracism of Aristides, at a date given variously between 485 and 482 BCE. A story told of the vote has an illiterate voter, who did not recognise Aristides, ask him to write the name Aristides on his shard; he said he did not know the man but was irritated to hear him everywhere called the Just. Aristides wrote his own name. Early in 480 BCE a decree recalling exiles let him help defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> against the Persian invaders, and he was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> for 480–479 BCE.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis">Battle of Salamis</a> in September 480 BCE he gave loyal support to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> and completed the victory by landing Athenian infantry on the island of Psyttaleia and destroying the Persian garrison there. Re-elected in 479 BCE, he commanded the Athenian forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a> in August with special powers. The Ionian allies, after revolting from the Spartan admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a>, gave him chief command and full discretion in fixing the contributions to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a>, formed in 478 BCE. His assessment was universally accepted as fair and remained the basis of taxation for most of the League's existence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Some authorities say Aristides died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and others that he died on a journey to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>; Nepos gives the date as 468 BCE. He lived to see the ostracism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>, to whom he always showed generosity, but died before the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a>. He apparently left too little to pay for his burial, and his descendants received state pensions even in the 4th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> called him the best and most honourable man in Athens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> wrote his life, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> praises him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Gorgias and Meno as an example of good leadership.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aristides” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Members swore an oath of loyalty, and the Athenian politician <b>Aristides</b> spent the rest of his life on the affairs of the alliance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Thebes produced the poet Pindar, the painters <b>Aristides</b> and Nicomachus and the Cynic philosopher Crates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Alopeke, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Classical Athens, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.95,23.75&amp;year=-490" aria-label="Alopeke on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5142 799 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aristides"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5592 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5592 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.95,23.75&amp;year=-490">What was here in 490 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
